Repo: /Users/marcel.a.n.navarro/Dev/zimmermann-pwa (branch feature/SFCC-11_cart, do not switch branches, do not commit anything).

CONTEXT: The working tree has uncommitted changes implementing a step that wires `ZimmQuantityStepper` into `overrides/app/components/product-item/index.tsx`. This is real, wanted, uncommitted work-in-progress — it is NOT something to discard. Your job is to mutation-test one specific function in it, then restore the file to its exact current state.

THE FUNCTION UNDER TEST, in `overrides/app/components/product-item/index.tsx` (currently around line 268-274):
```ts
  const handleStepperQuantityChange = (nextQuantity: number) => {
    void Promise.resolve(onItemQuantityChange(nextQuantity)).then(
      (isValidChange: boolean) => isValidChange && setQuantity(nextQuantity),
    );
  };
```
This wrapper is supposed to call the real basket-mutation prop `onItemQuantityChange` FIRST, and only commit to local state via `setQuantity` if that call resolves truthy. The whole point is that a wrong-but-tempting shortcut — wiring the stepper straight to `setQuantity` and skipping the real mutation — would make the UI look right while the real server-side cart silently stops updating. There are 5 new tests in `overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx` (in a `describe('handleStepperQuantityChange — real-mutation-then-conditional-local-update', ...)` block near the end of the file) that are supposed to catch exactly this regression if it were ever reintroduced.

SAFETY — READ BEFORE DOING ANYTHING:
- Do NOT run `git checkout --`, `git restore`, or `git stash` on this file or any file in the repo — the file's current uncommitted content is real work that must survive your test.
- The file currently has this exact fingerprint: MD5 583cc2fe6b4bcb3277e6fa93d8c99ede, 364 lines total, `git diff --stat -- overrides/app/components/product-item/index.tsx` reports "1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)". You will use this to verify you've restored it correctly at the end.
- Use the Edit tool (exact string match) to make your mutation and to reverse it — do not use `git` to revert this file.

YOUR TASK:
1. Run `npx vitest run overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx` first and confirm it currently passes in full (capture the real pass count — do not assume). Also run `npx tsc --noEmit` once at this point and note it's clean, as a baseline.
2. Using the Edit tool, change `handleStepperQuantityChange` to the wrong-shortcut version that bypasses the real mutation entirely:
```ts
  const handleStepperQuantityChange = (nextQuantity: number) => {
    setQuantity(nextQuantity);
  };
```
3. Run `npx vitest run overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx` again. Record exactly which tests fail (test names), the real failure output/assertion messages, and confirm the failure mechanism matches the prediction (i.e. the 5 new mutation-preservation tests fail because `onItemQuantityChange` is never called / because `setQuantity` is called with a value the mutation never validated — read the actual error text, don't assume). Also check whether any OTHER, pre-existing test in that file incidentally fails too (it's fine either way, just report what actually happens — e.g. the "omitting onItemQuantityChange" test might behave unexpectedly since the shortcut version never touches that prop at all).
4. Using the Edit tool, restore the exact original function body (the 4-line version shown above under "THE FUNCTION UNDER TEST").
5. Verify restoration is byte-exact: run `md5 overrides/app/components/product-item/index.tsx` and confirm it is exactly `583cc2fe6b4bcb3277e6fa93d8c99ede` again. Also run `git diff --stat -- overrides/app/components/product-item/index.tsx` and confirm it again reports "1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)". If either check fails to match, you have NOT correctly restored the file — fix it before finishing, by carefully diffing against what "THE FUNCTION UNDER TEST" section above shows as the correct content, and re-checking every other part of the file is untouched.
6. Run `npx vitest run overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx` one final time and confirm it's back to the exact same full-pass state as step 1.
7. Separately (no mutation needed, just reading and reasoning): check for stale-closure / React logic correctness issues in this wrapper and its surroundings in `product-item/index.tsx`. Specifically: (a) is `handleStepperQuantityChange` redefined fresh on every render (so it can't hold a stale `onItemQuantityChange`/`setQuantity` from a previous render), or is it memoized in a way that could go stale? (b) does it ever read `quantity` itself, or does it only ever receive an already-computed `nextQuantity` argument from the caller (`ZimmQuantityStepper`) — if the latter, there's no stale-`quantity`-closure risk in this function specifically, confirm that's actually true by reading `ZimmQuantityStepper`'s `handleDecrement`/`handleIncrement` in `overrides/app/components/ui-kits/molecules/zimmQuantityStepper/index.tsx` (computes `quantity - 1` / `quantity + 1` itself and passes the result). (c) is `stepQuantity` still destructured from `useDerivedProduct` anywhere in `product-item/index.tsx` even though it's no longer used by the new stepper (check if it's dead/unused now, which would be a TypeScript/lint concern, or if something else still reads it).

REPORT BACK (via your final message): the baseline pass count, the exact failing test names + failure mechanism observed when mutated, confirmation the file was restored byte-exact (with the actual md5/diff-stat output you got), the final re-confirmed pass count, and your findings on stale-closure/dead-variable risk from step 7. Be concrete — quote real command output, not paraphrases.

